nizations  and  from there you start to build it out  that's exactly what the us did putting  in a blanket ban before relaxing  regulations through vaccinations blood  tests and quarantines it's the scenario  lawyer camille lab chucks hopes plays  out in canada this fight is just  beginning the stakes are too high dogs  will literally suffer and die in even  greater numbers than they are now on the  streets in countries if the cfia doesn't  reverse course on this very poor  decision so far she says the cfia which  didn't respond to inquiries from global  news  isn't talking mike trelay global news  toronto  ahead the canadian made tools on the  world's largest space telescope  nasa's james webb telescope is the most  powerful telescope ever built it's given  us earthlings an unprecedented view of  the universe beaming back the clearest  and sharpest pictures of what's up in  space and as kylie stanton explains some  canadians played a key role in making it  possible  more than a century ago this technology  opened up the universe and opened  people's minds 1918 was when it was  completed scientists have been pushing  the limits of what's possible ever since  here we go with the release of these  pictures said to be worth a thousand  words they've been left almost  speechless kidney candy shop is the way  that i feel right now i'm honestly  amazed that it works because it's such a  complex piece of equipment and so much  has gone into it over so very many years  canadian scientists can take some of the  credit two critical components of the 10  billion dollar james webb telescope were  developed at the herzberg institute of  astrophysics in saanich the fine  guidance sensor allows the telescope to  find and focus on objects while the near  infrared imager and slitless  spectrograph helps to study the object  it finds so it's like you being in  toronto and you're blinking at me and i  can see it in montreal it is that  accurate that the our instrument has to  work  the whole process has been a practice in  patience the telescope was launched on  christmas day beginning its 1.5 million  kilometer trip into space james webb now  has its array out nearly seven months  later what it captured along the way  finally revealed there's just so much  going on here it's so beautiful but the  real payoff is yet to come canada will  receive a guaranteed share of webb's  observation time  450 hours for scientists here to study  the data collected over the next two  years one big project will be to look at  the the early universe we're going to  look at galaxy clusters and also we want  to study exoplanet atmospheres  but it's the sense of renewed wonder  that may have the most lasting impact as  we move through this century and beyond  it provides the excitement the interest  the hope that people need to carry on i  think the real the real benefit is in  the eyes of the people that learn from  it kelly stanton global news victoria  hot shot next the indigenous golf  champion blazing a trail  a woman from the sutena nation in  alberta is making a name for herself in  the golf world irene crowchild is a  two-time national drive champion and  she's going for her third title getting  to the top though hasn't been easy  heather urex west looks at crowchild's  journey from residential schools and her  hopes her success drives reconciliation  forward  her love of golf came later in life  at 16 she learned this was something she  could do  and at 26 she found she could be the  best  for me it's just like a surreal moment  i i remember in 2018  when they told me that i won like i just  like cried  it was crow child's first of two  national championship titles on the golf  range her swing is a thing of beauty  you know that ball is going far  but the danae woman from satina nation  south of calgary has come a long way too  i didn't know how to cope with like my  own childhood and  like  my own childhood trauma which like i  fell into addictions  it just  got to a point where i just like fell  off the deep end  crowchild was raised by her grandmother  a survivor of canada's residential  school system trauma that was passed  down through the generations until she  says it became too much to bear after  six years struggling with addiction  crowchild tried to take her own life it  was a wake-up call  i talked to my drug and alcohol  counselor like i just told him like  where's the papers  because i can't keep living like this i  am not going to be here  if i don't change  it was reconnecting with her indigenous  roots that crowchild says ultimately  saved her life she's now five and a half  years sober and ready for her biggest  year in the sport yet with an eye on a  third national title before heading to  the world championship later this year  in japan  but her biggest accomplishment to date  is being here  at all as an indigenous two-spirit woman  i do feel a lot of pride out on the golf  course because  an individual like me is not seeing  and while crowchild doesn't think of  herself as a role model she knows to her  community her achievements mean a lot  and so she drives  again and again  working to make the sport she loves more  inclusive and accessible while showing  that after trauma  there can be  success  heather urex west global news calgary  and that is global national for this  sunday night i'm nitu garcia tonight's  your canada is this old train bridge in  chitick lake saskatchewan thanks for  watching tracy tong will be at the  anchor desk tomorrow have a great night  [Music]
 

